<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">People's Republic of Bulgaria</h1><p class="st-meta">1946 CE – 1990 CE · capital Sofia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Communist Bulgaria, the Soviet Union's most loyal ally in Eastern Europe, ruled for 35 years by Todor Zhivkov.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Soviet army entered <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> in September 1944, and the communist-led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatherland-front-bulgaria">Fatherland Front</a> took power. Opponents were tried by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-court-bulgaria">People's Courts</a>, and a referendum in September 1946 abolished the monarchy of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-bulgaria">Kingdom of Bulgaria</a>. The People's Republic was proclaimed on 15 September 1946, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgi-dimitrov">Georgi Dimitrov</a> becoming prime minister in November, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dimitrov-constitution">Dimitrov Constitution</a> followed in 1947.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Communist Party nationalised industry, collectivised agriculture and crushed opposition, executing the Agrarian leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikola-petkov">Nikola Petkov</a> in 1947. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todor-zhivkov">Todor Zhivkov</a>, party leader from 1954, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> became the most faithful Soviet ally, industrialising rapidly with Soviet energy and markets. The 1984 to 1989 'Revival Process' forced Bulgarian Turks to take Slavic names, and in 1989 some 300,000 fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>On 10 November 1989, the day after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin-wall">Berlin Wall</a> opened, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/todor-zhivkov">Zhivkov</a> was ousted by reformers in his own party. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarian-round-table">Round Table</a> talks led to free elections in 1990, and in November 1990 the country was renamed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/bulgaria">Republic of Bulgaria</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/peoples-republic-of-bulgaria.jpg" alt="People's Republic of Bulgaria" width="320" height="192"><figcaption>Image: Scroch · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bulgaria_(1971%E2%80%931990).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1946 CE – 1990 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sofia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Georgi Dimitrov, Vulko Chervenkov, Todor Zhivkov</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>State atheism; Bulgarian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Bulgarian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Bulgaria</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Communist Bulgaria</dd></dl></aside></div>
